<h2>The tragedy at Harisal Range</h2>

<p>A Forest Range Officer posted at Harisal Range in Maharashtra died by suicide on 25 March 2021. She left behind notes alleging sustained ill-treatment by the Deputy Conservator of Forests under whom she served: repeated reprimands, rejection of leave applications, suspension of her work, an FIR registered against her, and — most seriously — that she had been made to undertake an arduous trek during pregnancy in October 2020, following which she suffered a miscarriage. On the strength of the notes, an FIR was registered against the officer under Sections 306, 504 and 506 of the IPC.</p>

<p>The officer sought discharge before the Sessions Court and failed. The High Court declined to interfere. Before the Supreme Court, the question was not whether the allegations were tragic — they plainly were — but whether, taken at their highest, they disclosed the offence of abetment of suicide.</p>

<h2>Three ingredients, strictly applied</h2>

<p>The Bench distilled the settled law on Section 306 into three cumulative requirements and tested the record against each.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep">
    <h3>1. Instigation or intentional aid</h3>
    <p>There must be an act of direct or indirect instigation, or intentional assistance, referable to the suicide. Administrative directions, adverse remarks on official performance and disciplinary supervision do not qualify — they are the ordinary incidents of a hierarchical service.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fstep">
    <h3>2. Proximity in time</h3>
    <p>The instigating act must stand in close proximity to the suicide. The Court noted that even where harassment is alleged to have continued over a long period, there must be a proximate prior act from which the suicide can be found to be the direct consequence. Here the gravest allegation — the trek and the miscarriage — dated to October 2020, months before the death in March 2021.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fstep">
    <h3>3. Mens rea</h3>
    <p>The accused must have intended, by his conduct, at least possibly to drive the victim to suicide. As the Court put it, the question is whether the accused intended by his action to at least possibly drive the victim to suicide — a state of mind that cannot be inferred merely from strict or even harsh supervision.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Measured against this framework, the material failed. The Court observed that merely because the victim was continuously harassed and at one point succumbed to suicide cannot by itself result in finding positive instigation constituting abetment. It also found the ingredients of Sections 504 (intentional insult) and 506 (criminal intimidation) unestablished. The orders of the Sessions Judge and the High Court were set aside and the appellant stood discharged from Sessions Case No. 52 of 2021.</p>

<h2>Why the judgment matters</h2>

<p>The decision does not dilute the seriousness of workplace harassment; it polices the boundary between a disciplinary wrong and a penal one. Three practical consequences follow for litigants in Delhi and elsewhere.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile">
    <h3>At the charge stage</h3>
    <p>Discharge is a real remedy in Section 306 prosecutions built on generalised workplace grievances. The charge court must find material for each ingredient — instigation, proximity, intent — not merely a narrative of friction.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile">
    <h3>For public servants</h3>
    <p>Supervisory acts done in the discharge of official duty — rejecting leave, recording adverse remarks, initiating proceedings — are not converted into criminal instigation by a later tragedy, absent proof of intent to bring it about.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile">
    <h3>Under the new codes</h3>
    <p>The same analysis governs Section 108 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, which replaces Section 306 IPC for offences after 1 July 2024. Investigations under the BNSS must gather material on proximity and mens rea, not only the suicide notes.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p>The judgment continues a consistent line of Supreme Court authority requiring a proximate, intentional act before Section 306 liability attaches, and applies it firmly at the discharge stage — sparing an accused a full trial where the ingredients are absent on the face of the record.</p>
</div>

<p>This discussion is informational and is not a substitute for advice on the facts of a particular case. This topic touches on suicide; readers affected by these issues can reach trained support in India through Tele-MANAS at 14416.</p>
